For convenience's sake, I've also copy/pasted it here:

ui <- shinyUI(fluidPage(
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectizeInput(
        inputId='id'
        ,choices = NULL
        ,label='id'
        ,multiple=T
      )
    )
    ,mainPanel(
      uiOutput(outputId='dynamicTabs')
      ,textOutput('testing')
    )
  )
))

server <- shinyServer(function(input, output, session) {
  updateSelectizeInput(
    session = session
    ,inputId = 'id'
    ,choices = row.names(mtcars)
    ,server = F
  )

  output$dynamicTabs <- renderUI({
    tabs <- lapply(input$id,function(x){
      tabPanel(
        title = paste0(x,'_Tab')
        ,h3(paste0('Tab showing:',x))
        ,textOutput(paste("The car you selected:", x,"has", mtcars[x,"cyl"], "cylinders")) ####### Comment this line and input$selectedTab outputs correctly
        ,value=x,
      )
    })
    do.call(tabsetPanel,c(tabs,id='selectedTab'))
  })

  output$testing <- renderText({
    input$selectedTab
  })

})

shinyApp(ui=ui,server=server)

Other information: I found this code via "https://github.com/rstudio/shiny/issues/924" but it was unresolved.

From u7b36a5521b8eb4d4 at groups.google.com  Fri Oct  9 05:00:11 2015
From: u7b36a5521b8eb4d4 at groups.google.com (Dario Strbenac)
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2015 05:00:11 +0000
Subject: renderPlot Parameters Processing Order
Message-ID: <enAXMsEruac.FefG8Xf-CQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/enAXMsEruac/m/FefG8Xf-CQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For renderPlot, the width and height parameters are processed before expr is. If I made a reactive expression for calculating it, I would need to copy some preliminary table filtering code twice, since when the reactive expression is called for width or height, it can't simply get variables referring to the filtered data table from renderPlot's expression, because the expression is not the parent environment in that case. Could renderPlot evaluate expr first, which would allow users to put the dimensions into the global environment and easily refer to them for width and height, if both of them were processed after expr ? Calling the reactive function twice is also unsuitable, for example, if it pops up a modal dialog message because all the of code was copied into the reactive expression, including the table subsetting code, which depends on at least one table row being selected, otherwise popping up an error message in a modal. If it were inside the reactive expression, it would annoyingly pop up twice to the user.

From uca443e6eaa0e78ed at groups.google.com  Fri Oct  9 05:04:22 2015
From: uca443e6eaa0e78ed at groups.google.com (Joe Cheng)
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2015 05:04:22 +0000
Subject: Re: renderPlot Parameters Processing Order
Message-ID: <enAXMsEruac.JS1qdbL-CQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <enAXMsEruac.FefG8Xf-CQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
References: <enAXMsEruac.FefG8Xf-CQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/enAXMsEruac/m/JS1qdbL-CQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The table filtering code should also be a reactive expression. And also, if a reactive expression is called twice without an intervening change to its reactive dependencies, then the second call will not cause the reactive expression code to be executed, because reactive expressions cache their results.

There's no way expr can execute before width/height. The graphics device must be initialized to the correct width/height before plotting code can execute.

